@incollection{Hemerijck.1999,
 abstract = {{\textquotedbl}Our theoretical propositions are the following. Policy learning is failure-induced. Fail-ures trigger responses by various actors that propose changes in the existing policy format. However, there is no guarantee that such policy recommendations will gain the political support necessary for  their adoption. Institutional change remains diffi-cult, but not impossible. For welfare reform it matters a great deal whether policy learning occurs in context where various policy domains are 'loosely' or 'tightly' cou-pled. We view policy learning as institutionally nested. Information about the institu-tional setting tells us something about the possible pace and character of policy change. Policy change in tightly coupled welfare states is necessarily 'negotiated' change. A broader conception of the welfare state, which acknowledges the exis-tence of several policy domains, and an institutionally sensitive theory of policy change will help us understand the variation in contemporary welfare state adjust-ment, but also in terms of changes in the form and extent of the coupling of policy domains.{\textquotedbl} (169)

Hall

{$\backslash$textquotedbl}The sequential nature of policy change is captured in terms of three orders of change, concerning (1) the overarching goals that guide policy in a particular field, (2) the techniques of policy instruments used to attain those goals, and (3) the precise setting of these instruments. Together these three elements define a policy para-digm. The ideas embodied in a policy paradigm have a status somewhat independ-ent of institutions. A policy-making process can be structured by a particular set of ideas, just as it can be structured by a set of institutions. Ideas in this context refer to a programmatic set of statements of cause and effect concerning specific policy problems together with a policy theory or method for influencing these causal rela-tionships with an eye on solving policy problems. When the overarching policy goals and assumptions about causes and effects are taken for granted, a policy paradigm is solidly anchored in accepted practices that link priorities, content, the institutions and the instruments of policy.{\textquotedbl} (177)

Sabatier's advocacy-coalitions

{$\backslash$textquotedbl}One of Sabatier's conclusions is clearly in agreement with Hall: an actor or coalition will give up secondary aspects of a belief system before acknowledging weaknesses in the policy core or policy paradigm. Therefore, Sabatier's model can best be ap-plied to highly technical and/or depoliticised policy areas, such as environmental protection, waste disposal, technical standardisation and health policy. However, the model seems to exclude a number of policy areas that are inherently more politi-cised, that is to say where 'raw' political power frequently guides the direction  of policy change. In addition, Sabatier's model seems most in tune with the pluralist change in political systems that have a more interventionist tradition - such as France - or a more consensual tradition - such as in the negotiating political systems of the Nordic countries, Austria, the Netherlands and Germany.{\textquotedbl} (179)

{\textquotedbl}For our purposes, Hall's learning approach has four considerable advantages over Sabatier's. First of all, Hall's comparative perspective guides the attention towards the extent to which institutional variables (e.g. the number of veto points, the distribu-tion of political power, the type of party government) shape policy change and learn-ing. Second, Hall's approach is more amenable to social and economic policy issues, which are inherently more politicised and which potentially spill over into electoral conflict. Third, the historical-institutionalist focus is particularly sensitive to the distinct impact of historical development of state formation and cleavage mobilisation on contemporary public policy, as these have structurally shaped administrative capaci-ties, electoral systems, and state-society relations. Finally, Hall in contrast to Saba-tier, urges us to take a broader perspective on learning as involving a wider range of public and media participation, with a far more open agenda. In short, Hall's perspec-tive enables us to study policy change without falling into the trap of the de-contextualised, de-politicised, de-historicised, and {\'e}litist conception of policy-making in much of contemporary policy network, policy community, subsystem and advocacy coalition studies.{\textquotedbl} (180)

{\textquotedbl}Welfare state reform is difficult, but it happens. We have identified some of the mechanisms: patching up institutions, transposing their use, and policy learning. We have argued that ideas and institutions are intimately related: institutions give us ideas; ideas are embodied in institutions. Another way of expressing this is that learning is institutionally nested. The institutional context of the welfare state is com-plex and consists of various policy domains, namely macroeconomic policy, industrial relations, social security and labour market policy. These domains very in policy content and are governed according to a different institutional logic. The relative ease and success of policy learning is contingent on the structure and character of the linkages between the different policy domains. This means that in some institutional contexts paradigmatic changes in ideas and policies happen more slowly and are less visible than in other contexts.{\textquotedbl} (182)},
 author = {Hemerijck, Anton and {van Kersbergen}, Kees},
 title = {Negotiated Policy Change: Towards a Theory of Institutional Learning in Tightly Coupled Welfare States},
 keywords = {adjustment;advocacy coalitions;ideas;institutional learning;policy paradigms},
 publisher = {{Edward Elgar}},
 editor = {Braun, Dietmar and Busch, Andreas},
 booktitle = {Public policy and political ideas},
 year = {1999},
 address = {Cheltenham u.a.}
}


