@article{Lange.1993,
 abstract = {THE DECEMBER 1991 SUMMIT OF THE EUROPEAN COMMUNITY (EC) AT MAASTRICHT UNDERTOOK A NUMBER OF INSTITUTIONAL REFORMS INCLUDING A {\textquotedbl}SOCIAL PROTOCOL{\textquotedbl} WHICH WOULD ALTER THE DECISION RULES COVERING ISSUES IN THE SO-CALLED SOCIAL DIMENSION TO ALLOW PASSAGE OF A WIDER RANGE OF SOCIAL POLICY MEASURES BY QUALIFIED MAJORITY. THE BEHAVIOR OF EC MEMBER STATES AT MAASTRICHT RAISES THE QUESTION: WHY DO SOVEREIGN STATES AGREE TO THE ESTABLISHMENT OF INSTITUTIONS AND DECISION RULES THAT ARE LIKELY TO ERODE THEIR SOVEREIGNTY AND /OR LEAD TO DECISIONS COSTLY TO THEM. THE AUTHOR OF THIS ESSAY EXAMINES THIS QUESTION BY LOOKING AT THE MAASTRICHT DECISIONS CONTAINED IN THE SOCIAL PROTOCOL. THE AUTHOR EXAMINES THE EXTENT TO WHICH THE PROVISIONS OF THE PROTOCOL REPRESENT A SIGNIFICANT STEP TOWARD THE SUBSTITUTION OF COLLECTIVE EUROPEAN OVER SOVEREIGN NATIONAL DECISION MAKING IN THE DEVELOPMENT OF A EUROPEAN SOCIAL POLICY. HE THEN EXAMINES THE SOCIAL PROTOCOL AS AN EXAMPLE OF INSTITUTIONAL CHANGE AND EXPLORES THE LOGIC OF THE DECISION MAKING OF THE MEMBER STATES. HE CONCLUDES THAT THE EXPANSION OF EC COMPETENCIES IN THE SOCIAL DIMENSION IS LINKED TO AND DEPENDENT ON PROGRESS IN OTHER, MORE IMPORTANT POLICY AREAS.},
